C o m p i l a t i o n


      CEE 6410 - Water Resources Systems Analysis
      Example 2.1 from Bishop Et Al Text (https://digitalcommons.usu.edu/ecstatic_all/76/)
      Modifies Example to add a labor constraint
       
      THE PROBLEM:
       
      An irrigated farm can be planted in two crops:  eggplants and tomatoes.  Data are as fol-lows:
       
      Seasonal Resource
      Inputs or Profit        Crops        Resource
      Availability
              Eggplant        Tomatoes
      Water        1x103 gal/plant        2x103 gal/plant      4x106 gal/year
      Land        4 ft2/plant        3 ft2/plant               1.2x104 ft2
      Labor         5hr/plant        2.5/hr plant              17,500 hours
      Profit/plant        $6        $7
       
                      Determine the optimal planting for the two crops.
       
      THE SOLUTION:
      Uses General Algebraic Modeling System to Solve this Linear Program
       
      David E Rosenberg
      david.rosenberg@usu.edu
      September 15, 2015
  28   
  29  * 1. DEFINE the SETS
  30  SETS plnt crops growing /Hay, Grain/
  31       res resources /June, July, August, Land/;
  32   
  33  * 2. DEFINE input data
  34  PARAMETERS
  35     c(plnt) Objective function coefficients ($ per acre of crop)
  36           /Hay 100,
  37          Grain 120 /
  38   
  39     b(res) Right hand constraint values (per resource)
  40            /June 14000,
  41             July  18000,
  42             August 6000,
  43             Land   10000/;
  44   
  45  TABLE A(plnt,res) Left hand side constraint coefficients
  46              June    July     August   Land
  47   Hay          2      1          1       1
  48   Grain        1      2          0       1;
  49   
  50   
  51  * 3. DEFINE the variables
  52  VARIABLES X(plnt) plants planted (Number)
  53            VPROFIT  total profit ($)
  54            Y(res) value of resources used
  55            VREDCOST total reduced cost;
  56   
  57  * Non-negativity constraints
  58  POSITIVE VARIABLES X, Y;
  59   
  60  * 4. COMBINE variables and data in equations
  61  EQUATIONS
  62     PROFIT Total profit ($) and objective function value
  63     RES_CONSTRAIN(res) Resource Constraints
  64     REDCOST_DUAL Reduced Cost ($) associated with using resources
  65     RES_CONS_DUAL(plnt) Profit levels ;
  66   
  67  PROFIT..                 VPROFIT =E= SUM(plnt, c(plnt)*X(plnt));
  68  RES_CONSTRAIN(res) ..    SUM(plnt, A(plnt,res)*X(plnt)) =L= b(res);
  69   
  70  *Dual Equations
  71  REDCOST_DUAL..                 VREDCOST =E= SUM(res,b(res)*Y(res));
  72  RES_CONS_DUAL(plnt)..          sum(res,A(plnt,res)*Y(res)) =G= c(plnt);
  73   
  74  * 5. DEFINE the MODEL from the EQUATIONS
  75  MODEL PLANTING_PRIMAL /PROFIT, RES_CONSTRAIN/;
  76  MODEL PLANTING_DUAL /REDCOST_DUAL, RES_CONS_DUAL/
  77  *Altnerative way to write (include all previously defined equations)
  78  *MODEL PLANTING /ALL/;
  79   
  80   
  81  * 6. SOLVE the MODEL
  82  * Solve the PLANTING model using a Linear Programming Solver (see File=>Options=>Solvers)
  83  *     to maximize VPROFIT
  84  SOLVE PLANTING_DUAL USING LP MINIMIZING VREDCOST;
  85   
  86   
  87  * 6. CLick File menu => RUN (F9) or Solve icon and examine solution report in .LST file


